cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "Donghyunhang (동현항): a single-player sailing and trading simulation game on a world map with hex sea battles, with starting values and rule numbers set through the esoteric language Donghyun-lang"
type: software
version: "1.33"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24106054"
date-released: "2026-11-25"
authors:
  - family-names: Kim
    given-names: Dong Hyun
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9883-0229"
    affiliation: "Inha University College of Medicine"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Raspberry-Kim/donghyun-lab17"
url: "https://raspberry-kim.github.io/donghyun-lab17/"
abstract: >-
  Version 1.33 corrects the ordinal of this lab in the README
  (eighteenth in the series, counting the Donghyunlang site first).
  Rules, data, examples and saves remain unchanged.
  Version 1.32 rewords messages, seven Korean renderings of the
  adviser's quotations, pages and notes as plain statements. Rules, data,
  examples and saves remain unchanged.
  Version 1.31 moves the Donghyunte link, which stood apart at the end
  of the footer, into the same list as the other series links. Rules,
  data, examples and saves remain unchanged.
  Version 1.30 added a desktop-recommended notice box above the world
  map, the same box as in Donghyunsam: a pastel coral border that blinks
  ten times after the page opens and then stays lit. Rules, data,
  examples and saves remain unchanged.
  Version 1.29 added a victory celebration: on winning by either ending, a
  synthesized fanfare of about ten seconds plays the first line of the
  trio tune of Elgar's Pomp and Circumstance March No. 1 (public domain;
  no recording is used) while pixel flower petals fall over the map,
  leaving the result card clear. It also fixes the duchy ending, which
  played the defeat sound although the game was won. Rules, data,
  examples and saves remain unchanged.
  Version 1.28 fixes three source links on the page that were cut at a
  parenthesis inside the address (Wikipedia articles on Butuan, the
  capture of Algiers and James Taylor), replaces one source that is no
  longer online with its archived copy, and removes an outdated test
  script. Game rules, data, examples and saves remain unchanged.
  Version 1.27 gives each of the 45 ports its own collaborator portrait
  by adding 36 new pixel faces (previously 11 faces were shared by three
  to five ports each). Jemulpo and Nagasaki have two collaborators who
  alternate with the visit count already stored in saves. Trading rules,
  market advice, data and save compatibility remain unchanged.
  Version 1.26 redraws the small map sprite of the Aegis ship in the same
  aspect ratio as the battle sprite (21x15 cells, width-to-height 1.40
  against 1.42 in battle; previously 19x12 and 1.67), with a deeper hull,
  a lowered stern deck and a clearer helicopter. The battle sprite,
  colors, combat statistics and save compatibility remain unchanged.
  Version 1.25 repaints the Aegis ship's hull, superstructure and barrel
  in a darker neutral gray so that the hull separates from the sea colors
  (hull-to-sea luminance contrast 1.9-2.5, previously 1.3-1.8). Sprite
  layout, animation, combat statistics and save compatibility remain
  unchanged.
  Version 1.24 redraws the Aegis ship as a gray warship with a navy
  outline, hull number 997, a stern helicopter and flagstaff, and a round
  turret. The hull number reads left to right in both directions and the
  flagship flies the Taegeukgi; combat statistics, 90-tick firing duration
  and save compatibility remain unchanged.
  Version 1.23 adopts a rounded honey-colored wooden Aegis sprite with
  a centered bridge, a separate forward cannon and a deck-mounted flagstaff.
  The map and battle sprites share the approved design; combat statistics,
  90-tick firing duration and save compatibility remain unchanged.
  Version 1.22 adds a shorter 45-tick panokseon firing animation with
  smaller flames and removes hue filters from all port portraits,
  preserving combat rules, portrait artwork and save compatibility.
  Version 1.21 aligns the Aegis ship's bow and cannon with its travel
  direction during sailing, battle setup and firing.
  Version 1.20 refines wooden Aegis and panokseon sprites, extends
  special cannon effects to 90 ticks, animates the turtle ship's jaws,
  and adds 100 source-linked Schopenhauer passages with polite character
  dialogue. Version 1.00 introduced port investment and cooperative-port benefits,
  trade, combat and exploration fame, six simplified noble ranks,
  three ending modes, computed market advice and original face portraits.
  A turtle flagship, panokseon and caravel start with generous funds;
  an anachronistic Aegis ship is available at duke rank.
  Donghyunhang is a browser-based, noncommercial toy that follows only the
  broad frame of Uncharted Waters II (Daikoukai Jidai II; Koei, 1993): a
  captain leads a fleet across a 16th-century world map, buying low and
  selling high among forty-five ports that trade forty-seven goods in nine
  kinds. At sea the calendar advances one day per step while food and water
  run down; in port the player uses a market, a lodge, a shipyard, a supply
  menu, a guild with delivery contracts and a cargo hold sorted by kind.
  Meeting one of eight pirate bands, five of which keep to their own seas,
  offers five choices (fight, flee, pay a toll, pay in cargo, scare them
  off); fighting opens an 8x8 hex sea battle with cannon fire, boarding and
  a captains' duel, and captured ships can join the fleet or be sold. The captain is Podongi, the maker's Maltese dog. Starting values,
  rule numbers, the victory condition and time limit are set through a
  program written in the esoteric programming language Donghyun-lang, whose
  integer output is read as pairs of setting number and value by a shared
  adapter. The game runs on a small common engine (fixed 60-tick loop,
  calendar, square and hex grids with A* path finding, tile map renderer,
  scene stack) written in HTML5 Canvas and vanilla JavaScript and shared
  with Donghyunsam. The world map is derived from Natural Earth 110m land
  data (public domain) at one degree per tile, with four narrow straits
  opened by hand; prices, ships, rival fleets and all rule numbers are test
  values chosen for this work and were not checked against the original
  game. No graphic, sound, text, number or code of the original game is used
  or extracted. Uncharted Waters is a series of Koei Tecmo Games; this work
  is unaffiliated. Input is keyboard, mouse or touch, with a fullscreen
  mode. A voyage can be saved by hand to three browser slots or to a .dhsave
  file and loaded back, together with the applied settings, the calendar,
  the random-number state and the market prices. The whole game can be
  played by keyboard: numbered control buttons, arrow keys to pick ports in
  harbour or to steer at sea, and a hex cursor in sea battles.
  Sound effects are synthesized in the browser with the Web Audio API (no
  audio files) and can be switched off with a header button or the M key.
  A start card waits on the map until the player presses Sortie. The map
  shades the sea in three depths by distance from shore. Small passing
  ships (coastal boats, regional merchantmen and explorers) move around the
  view without fighting and open a picture and description when clicked.
  A history check in the README compares the 1522 setting of ports, local
  goods and pirates with cited sources in five groups (matches, partly
  matches, later than 1522 and placed on purpose, does not match, not
  found); places and goods that do not fit 1522 are kept on purpose for
  play.
  The MIT license applies to the Donghyunhang code and documentation; the
  page fonts are under the SIL Open Font License. See LICENSE and
  CREDITS.md.
keywords:
